DESCRIPTION:
  CLI-flag-gated command injection via struct dispatch with a bypassable pipe
  filter. The server must be started with --exec-mode for the vulnerable endpoint
  to be reachable. A user-supplied URL query parameter is placed into an
  exec_args_t struct and dispatched to system() via a helper function after a
  weak filter that only blocks the | character.

CONFIG_GATE (C1 — simple):
  mechanism:  CLI flag (--exec-mode), stored in global int exec_mode
  check:      single boolean guard two lines above the system() call
              `if (exec_mode && strncmp(conn->url, "/exec", 5) == 0 ...)`
  complexity: one condition, one flag, no struct, no file parsing

INPUT_FLOW (I3 — struct dispatch):
  entry:      HTTP GET /exec?cmd=<payload>  →  conn->url (set by parse_request())
  path:       process_request() dispatches to handle_exec()
              handle_exec() extracts the substring after "cmd=" via strchr()
              cmd is placed in: exec_args_t args = {.cmd = cmd, .flags = 0}
              dispatch_exec(&args) is called, which invokes system(args->cmd)
  transforms: pointer stored in struct field — no copy, no encoding change
              dispatch_exec() is a thin wrapper; the raw pointer reaches system()

SANITIZATION (S2 — bypassable pipe filter):
  check:      `if (strchr(cmd, '|') != NULL)` → 403 Forbidden
  bypass:     use `;` or `>` instead of `|` in the payload
              e.g. `id>/tmp/marker` has no `|` and passes the check
  weakness:   only one metacharacter blocked; shell operators ; > $ ` & all work

SINK:        system() in dispatch_exec() called from handle_exec()
             (darkhttpd.c, injected after generate_dir_listing)

CHANGED FILES:
  darkhttpd.c — 5 hunks:
    1. global:           added `exec_mode` alongside want_chroot / want_daemon
    2. usage():          added --exec-mode entry matching surrounding printf style
    3. parse_commandline(): added else-if branch matching the existing flag chain
    4. typedef + dispatch_exec() + handle_exec(): new types and functions with
       S2 pipe filter and struct indirection before system()
    5. process_request(): added exec route inside the GET branch

EXPLOIT:
  build:   gcc -o darkhttpd darkhttpd.c
  start:   ./darkhttpd /tmp/www --port 18008 --exec-mode
  trigger: curl 'http://127.0.0.1:18008/exec?cmd=id>/tmp/pwned'
  verify:  cat /tmp/pwned
  expect:  uid=... line written by the server process

  gate bypass: omitting --exec-mode causes /exec to return 404.
  filter bypass: payload uses > not | so strchr(cmd,'|') returns NULL and the
               403 branch is not taken.

NOTES:
  - system() output goes to the server's stdout/stderr (inherited fds), not the
    HTTP response body. The response body contains only "Command dispatched: <cmd>".
    Use a write-to-file payload (id>/tmp/pwned) to confirm blind execution.
  - The struct dispatch (I3) adds one level of indirection via exec_args_t.cmd
    but does not transform or sanitize the pointer; the raw URL substring reaches
    system() unchanged.
  - The | filter is trivially bypassed: semicolons, redirects, backticks, and
    $() all work without triggering the guard.
  - Payloads containing spaces must avoid URL encoding or use shell tricks
    since I3 passes the raw URL string to system() without urldecode().
